/*
RMSTEST_32 - does the elevation diagonal step hold at another map size, with
             player lands present
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 11(b), Sec.6.2

QUESTION
RMSTEST_22a/22b overturned Sec.6.2 completely: DE renders the map rotated 45
degrees, so the guide's "south" is the y-x diagonal, and the bias is a STEP
(18:1, softening to 12:1 with enable_balanced_elevation) rather than the ramp
every revision through rev 6 modelled. No linear weight could ever have fitted
it. That result is not in doubt.

What is unmeasured is its scope. Both runs were on ONE map size (120) with NO
player lands, and the second of those was deliberate: Sec.6.2 excludes tiles
within 9 of a player origin, and excluding lands kept the distribution clean
enough to see the step at all. But player rings sit on a CIRCLE, and a circle
crosses the diagonal. The interaction between that exclusion and the half-map
step is exactly the thing a clean-room measurement cannot show, and 4.2 is about
to rely on it.

METHOD
RMSTEST_22a's elevation block unchanged, at a different map size, with player
lands present. Two variables move at once, which is normally wrong - here it is
deliberate, because each has its own signature in the output and a single run
answers both:
  a size change shifts WHERE the step falls (it should stay on y = x, i.e. at
    the map's own diagonal, not at a fixed tile count)
  the player lands punch HOLES in the distribution at known radii, without
    moving the step
If the step moves, that is the size result. If the step blurs or tilts, that is
the interaction result. They do not look alike.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH 8 PLAYERS. Normal rather than Tiny so the step
sits at a different absolute tile coordinate (99.5 rather than 59.5) - a rule
expressed in tiles and a rule expressed as a fraction of the map predict
different things here, and on Tiny alone they were indistinguishable.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. The step is still at the y = x diagonal, now at the 200 map's own centre
   line. If instead it stays near tile 60, the rule is in absolute tiles and was
   only ever fitted to Tiny.
2. The ratio across the step stays near 18:1. A materially different ratio at a
   different size means the bias strength scales with the map, which nothing
   currently models.
3. The player-land exclusion shows up as eight low-elevation holes on the ring,
   NOT as a change in the step. If the disfavoured half's elevation count rises
   where the ring crosses the diagonal, the exclusion and the step interact and
   Sec.6.2 needs both rules stated together rather than composed independently.
4. Item 11(a)'s unexplained residual - the disfavoured half's internal gradient
   running the wrong way, 0.09 next to the diagonal against 0.21 in the far
   corner - should reappear here if it is real. It is only about 6 percent of
   elevated tiles so it does not move the fit, but reproducing it at a second
   size is what would turn it from a curiosity into something to model. If it
   does NOT reappear, it was a Tiny-specific artefact and item 11(a) can close.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows ELEVATION --bands 20
  Twenty bands rather than the default ten, because the question is now about
  structure WITHIN each half, not just the ratio between them.
  Also run --patches GRASS to locate the eight player lands, so the holes in the
  elevation distribution can be attributed rather than guessed at.

RUN IT THREE TIMES.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    number_of_tiles 200
    base_size 4
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 7
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 60
    number_of_tiles 2000
    set_scale_by_size
  }

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
